interviewQuestions.answer:
Both `&&` and `||` stop evaluating as soon as the overall result is determined, rather than always evaluating both operands. This is called short-circuiting.

- `&&` returns the first falsy operand it encounters, or the last operand if all are truthy. This is commonly used to conditionally run code only when a condition holds: isLoggedIn && renderDashboard().
- `||` returns the first truthy operand, or the last operand if all are falsy. This is commonly used for default values (with the caveats described for nullish coalescing): const name = input || 'Anonymous'.

```js
function renderDashboard() {
  console.log('Dashboard rendered');
  return true;
}

const isLoggedIn = false;
isLoggedIn && renderDashboard(); // renderDashboard() is never called
```

Short-circuiting is not just a performance optimization. It is often used deliberately to guard an expression, ensuring a function call or property access only happens when a preceding condition is true, which avoids errors from calling methods on undefined values.

codeExamples.code:
const name = 'Maya';
const age = 29;
console.log(`${name} is ${age} years old.`);
console.log(`Next year ${name} will be ${age + 1}.`);

const message = `Line one
Line two
Line three`;
console.log(message);

