## Conditional Branching: if/else and switch

Conditional statements let a program execute different code paths based on a condition. The most fundamental form is `if`, optionally followed by one or more `else if` clauses and a final `else`.

```js
function classify(score) {
  if (score >= 90) {
    return 'A';
  } else if (score >= 80) {
    return 'B';
  } else {
    return 'C or below';
  }
}
```

Each condition is evaluated top to bottom, and the first truthy branch wins. Later branches are skipped entirely once a match is found.

When you are comparing a single value against many possible exact matches, a `switch` statement is often more readable than a long if/else if chain:

```js
switch (day) {
  case 'Mon':
  case 'Tue':
  case 'Wed':
  case 'Thu':
  case 'Fri':
    console.log('Weekday');
    break;
  case 'Sat':
  case 'Sun':
    console.log('Weekend');
    break;
  default:
    console.log('Unknown day');
}
```

Important details about switch:

- Comparisons use strict equality (===), so type coercion does not happen.
- Without a break statement, execution falls through into the next case, sometimes intentional (as shown by stacking case Mon through case Fri), but a common source of bugs when forgotten.
- default runs when no case matches, and can be placed anywhere, though convention puts it last.

Choose if/else for ranges and complex boolean logic, and switch for a single value matched against discrete constants.

codeExamples.code:
function classify(score) {
  if (score >= 90) {
    return 'A';
  } else if (score >= 80) {
    return 'B';
  } else {
    return 'C or below';
  }
}
console.log(classify(95));
console.log(classify(82));
console.log(classify(60));

function dayType(day) {
  switch (day) {
    case 'Sat':
    case 'Sun':
      return 'Weekend';
    default:
      return 'Weekday';
  }
}
console.log(dayType('Sun'));
console.log(dayType('Tue'));

const values = [0, '', null, undefined, NaN, '0', [], {}];
for (const v of values) {
  console.log(Boolean(v));
}

function greet(name) {
  const displayName = name || 'Guest';
  return `Hello, ${displayName}!`;
}
console.log(greet('Lena'));
console.log(greet(''));
console.log(greet());

interviewQuestions.answer:
if/else evaluates arbitrary boolean conditions and is best suited for ranges, compound logic, or conditions that are not simple equality checks. switch compares a single expression against a list of exact values using strict equality (===), and reads more clearly than a long if/else if chain when you have many discrete cases to match against the same value.

```js
// if/else suits a range check:
if (score >= 90) { grade = 'A'; }
else if (score >= 80) { grade = 'B'; }

// switch suits discrete, exact matches:
switch (status) {
  case 'pending':
  case 'processing':
    label = 'In progress';
    break;
  case 'done':
    label = 'Complete';
    break;
  default:
    label = 'Unknown';
}
```

A good rule of thumb: reach for switch when you are checking one variable against three or more specific values, and if/else for everything else, especially ranges or combined conditions involving && or ||.

JavaScript defines exactly eight falsy values: false, 0, -0, 0n, '' (empty string), null, undefined, and NaN. Every other value is truthy, including '0', 'false' (non-empty strings), and, somewhat counterintuitively, empty arrays [] and empty objects {}.

```js
if ([]) console.log('arrays are truthy, even empty ones');
if ({}) console.log('objects are truthy, even empty ones');
if ('0') console.log('non-empty strings are truthy, even the string zero');
```

Arrays and objects are truthy regardless of their contents because truthiness for objects is based on type, not content. Any object reference, which includes arrays since arrays are objects, is truthy. This differs from languages like Python, where an empty list or dict is falsy, which is a common source of confusion for developers coming from those languages.

A switch statement executes every case clause from the first match onward until it hits a break, return, or the end of the switch block. It does not automatically stop after one matching case. Forgetting a break is one of the most common switch bugs, silently running code meant for a different case.

```js
switch (role) {
  case 'admin':
    console.log('Full access');
    // missing break!
  case 'editor':
    console.log('Edit access');
    break;
  default:
    console.log('Read-only access');
}
// role === 'admin' logs BOTH 'Full access' AND 'Edit access'
```

Ways to avoid this:

- Always include a break (or return, if inside a function) at the end of every case, even the last one, as a defensive habit.
- Use intentional fallthrough only when stacking multiple case labels for the same logic, as in case Sat and case Sun, and consider a comment noting it is intentional when a case genuinely falls through to the next block's logic.
- Consider linting rules, like no-fallthrough in ESLint, that flag missing breaks automatically.
- For complex branching logic, an object/map lookup or if/else chain is sometimes clearer and avoids fallthrough risk entirely.

