## for, while, and do...while Loops

JavaScript provides several ways to repeat code. The classic for loop is ideal when you know in advance how many iterations you need, or when you need direct access to an index:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  console.log(`Iteration ${i}`);
}
```

It has three parts separated by semicolons: an initializer (let i = 0), a condition checked before each iteration (i < 3), and an update expression run after each iteration (i++).

A while loop repeats as long as a condition remains true, checked before each iteration, so the body might never run at all if the condition starts false:

```js
let n = 5;
while (n > 0) {
  n--;
}
```

A do...while loop is similar, but checks the condition after running the body, guaranteeing the body executes at least once:

```js
let attempts = 0;
do {
  attempts++;
} while (attempts < 0); // body still runs once even though condition is false
```

Choosing between them:

- Use for when the number of iterations or an index is known.
- Use while when you are looping based on a condition that might never be true.
- Use do...while when the loop body must run at least once regardless of the condition, such as prompting a user at least one time.

All three loop types support break (exit the loop immediately) and continue (skip to the next iteration), covered in more depth in the next lesson.

codeExamples.code:
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  console.log(`for: ${i}`);
}

let n = 3;
while (n > 0) {
  console.log(`while: ${n}`);
  n--;
}

let attempts = 0;
do {
  attempts++;
  console.log(`Attempt number ${attempts}`);
} while (attempts < 0);
console.log(`Final attempts: ${attempts}`);

