## for...of, for...in, and Loop Control

Two specialized loop forms make iterating over collections more expressive than a manual index-based for loop.

for...of iterates over the values of any iterable: arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, and more.

```js
for (const char of 'abc') {
  console.log(char);
}
```

for...in iterates over the enumerable property keys of an object (including, for arrays, their numeric indices as strings). It is designed for plain objects, not arrays. Using it on an array can include inherited or unexpected enumerable properties and does not guarantee numeric order across engines, so prefer for...of or array methods for arrays.

```js
const user = { name: 'Kai', role: 'admin' };
for (const key in user) {
  console.log(`${key}: ${user[key]}`);
}
```

Loop control statements work inside any loop:

- break exits the loop entirely, immediately.
- continue skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next one.

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  if (i === 2) continue; // skip 2
  if (i === 4) break;    // stop entirely at 4
  console.log(i);
}
```

A good rule of thumb: reach for for...of when you need values from an array or iterable, for...in only when you specifically need an object's own enumerable keys, often combined with Object.hasOwn to filter out inherited properties, and break/continue whenever you need fine-grained control over which iterations execute.

codeExamples.code:
const colors = ['red', 'green', 'blue'];
for (const color of colors) {
  console.log(color);
}

const point = { x: 1, y: 2 };
for (const key in point) {
  console.log(`${key}=${point[key]}`);
}

for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
  if (i % 2 === 0) continue;
  if (i === 5) break;
  console.log(i);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
for...of iterates over the values produced by an iterable: arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, and other objects that implement the iterable protocol. for...in iterates over the enumerable property keys of an object, including inherited enumerable properties from its prototype chain.

```js
const arr = ['a', 'b', 'c'];
for (const value of arr) console.log(value);  // 'a', 'b', 'c'
for (const key in arr) console.log(key);       // '0', '1', '2' -- as strings!

const obj = { x: 1, y: 2 };
for (const key in obj) console.log(key);       // 'x', 'y'
// for (const value of obj) -- throws: obj is not iterable
```

The practical rule: use for...of for arrays and other iterables when you want the actual values, and use for...in only for plain objects when you specifically need their keys, never for arrays, since it iterates indices as strings, may include unexpected inherited properties, and does not guarantee numeric order across all engines.

A while loop checks its condition before running the body, so the body may never execute if the condition starts out false. A do...while loop checks the condition after running the body, guaranteeing it executes at least once, regardless of the initial condition.

This matters whenever the first iteration must always happen before you have enough information to evaluate the condition meaningfully. A classic example is retry logic or input prompting, where you need to attempt the action once before deciding whether to repeat it:

```js
let input;
let attempts = 0;
do {
  input = getUserInput(); // must try at least once
  attempts++;
} while (!isValid(input) && attempts < 3);
```

If this were written as a plain while loop, you would need to duplicate the body, once before the loop to get an initial value, and once inside, which is exactly the duplication do...while avoids.

Because var is function-scoped rather than block-scoped, a single var loop variable is shared across every iteration of a loop. If you create closures, like setTimeout callbacks, inside the loop that reference that variable, they all end up referencing the same variable, whose final value is whatever it was after the loop finished, not the value at the time each closure was created.

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// logs: 3, 3, 3 -- not 0, 1, 2!
```

let fixes this because it creates a new binding for each iteration of the loop. Each closure captures its own separate i:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// logs: 0, 1, 2 -- as expected
```

Before let existed, the standard fix was an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) that captured the current value of i as a new parameter in its own function scope for each iteration:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function (capturedI) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(capturedI), 0);
  })(i);
}
// logs: 0, 1, 2
```

This is a frequently asked interview question because it tests understanding of closures, scope, and how ES2015's let changed loop semantics specifically to solve this exact problem.

