## Function Declarations, Expressions, and Parameters

JavaScript offers multiple ways to define a function, and each has different behavior around hoisting.

A function declaration is fully hoisted. Both its name and its implementation are available anywhere in the enclosing scope, even before the line where it is written:

```js
sayHi(); // works, logs 'Hi!'
function sayHi() {
  console.log('Hi!');
}
```

A function expression assigns an (often anonymous) function to a variable. Only the variable binding is hoisted (if declared with var), not the function body, so calling it before the assignment throws an error:

```js
const sayBye = function () {
  console.log('Bye!');
};
```

Modern JavaScript also supports flexible parameter handling:

- Default parameters provide a fallback when an argument is omitted or undefined: function greet(name = 'Guest') { ... }.
- Rest parameters collect any remaining arguments into a real array using ...: function sum(...numbers) { ... }.

```js
function describe(name, role = 'member', ...tags) {
  return `${name} (${role}): ${tags.join(', ')}`;
}
```

Default parameters are evaluated left-to-right and can reference earlier parameters. Rest parameters must always come last, and unlike the old arguments object, they are real arrays with full access to array methods like map and reduce.

codeExamples.code:
function describe(name, role = 'member', ...tags) {
  return `${name} (${role}): ${tags.join(', ')}`;
}
console.log(describe('Priya'));
console.log(describe('Sam', 'admin', 'backend', 'lead'));

console.log(typeof sayHi);
function sayHi() {
  return 'Hi!';
}

console.log(typeof sayBye);
var sayBye = function () {
  return 'Bye!';
};

