## Scope, Hoisting, and Arrow Functions

Scope determines where a variable is accessible. JavaScript has three main levels:

- Global scope - accessible everywhere in the program.
- Function scope - variables declared with var (or function parameters) are visible throughout the entire function.
- Block scope - variables declared with let/const are confined to the nearest { } block.

Hoisting means declarations are processed before code executes. var declarations and function declarations are hoisted with their value or implementation (for functions) or undefined (for var). let/const are hoisted too, but remain in an uninitialized temporal dead zone until their declaration line actually runs.

Arrow functions (=>), introduced in ES2015, offer a concise syntax and one crucial behavioral difference from regular functions: they do not have their own this. Instead, an arrow function captures this lexically, from the surrounding scope at the time it is defined, rather than depending on how it is called.

```js
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds++; // this correctly refers to the Timer instance
    }, 1000);
  }
}
```

If start() had used a regular function instead of an arrow function for the setInterval callback, this inside that callback would no longer refer to the Timer instance, because regular functions determine this based on how they are called, not where they are defined. This lexical this behavior is the single biggest reason arrow functions are preferred for callbacks inside methods.

Arrow functions also cannot be used as constructors (no new), and they do not have their own arguments object.

codeExamples.code:
const counter = {
  count: 0,
  incrementWithArrow() {
    const arrow = () => {
      this.count++;
    };
    arrow();
  },
};
counter.incrementWithArrow();
counter.incrementWithArrow();
console.log(counter.count);

function outer() {
  let outerVar = 'value from outer scope';
  function inner() {
    console.log(outerVar);
  }
  inner();
}
outer();

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(`let i: ${i}`), 0);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
Regular functions determine their this value dynamically, based on how they are called: as a method (obj.method()), a plain function call, with call/apply/bind, or as a constructor (new Fn()). Arrow functions have no this of their own at all. Instead, they capture this lexically from the enclosing scope at the time they are defined, and that binding never changes no matter how the arrow function is later invoked.

This distinction matters most inside callbacks within class methods or object methods, where you want this to keep referring to the instance:

```js
class Counter {
  constructor() {
    this.count = 0;
  }
  startAutoIncrement() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.count++; // arrow function: this is the Counter instance
    }, 1000);
  }
}
```

If startAutoIncrement used a regular function for the setInterval callback instead of an arrow function, this inside that callback would no longer refer to the Counter instance. setInterval invokes the callback as a plain function call, so this would be undefined (in strict mode / ES modules) or the global object (in loose mode), breaking this.count++. Before arrow functions existed, the common workaround was assigning const self = this outside the callback, or using .bind(this).

A closure is formed when a function remembers the variables from the scope in which it was created, even after that outer scope has finished executing. In JavaScript, every function forms a closure over its surrounding lexical scope, which is why inner functions can keep accessing outer variables long after the outer function has returned.

```js
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return function () {
    count++; // closes over count from makeCounter's scope
    return count;
  };
}
const counter = makeCounter();
counter(); // 1
counter(); // 2 -- count persisted between calls
```

Currying transforms a function that takes multiple arguments into a sequence of functions that each take one argument (or a subset), relying entirely on closures to remember the arguments collected so far:

```js
function curry(fn) {
  return function curried(...args) {
    if (args.length >= fn.length) {
      return fn.apply(this, args);
    }
    return (...moreArgs) => curried.apply(this, [...args, ...moreArgs]);
  };
}

function add3(a, b, c) { return a + b + c; }
const curriedAdd = curry(add3);
curriedAdd(1)(2)(3); // 6
curriedAdd(1, 2)(3);  // 6
```

Each returned function closes over the args accumulated so far, which is exactly what lets the curried function remember partial arguments across multiple calls.

codeExamples.code:
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
const result = numbers
  .filter(n => n % 2 === 0)
  .map(n => n * n);
console.log(JSON.stringify(result));

const orders = [
  { category: 'fruit', amount: 10 },
  { category: 'veg', amount: 5 },
  { category: 'fruit', amount: 7 },
];

const total = orders.reduce((sum, order) => sum + order.amount, 0);
console.log(total);

const grouped = orders.reduce((acc, order) => {
  acc[order.category] = (acc[order.category] || 0) + order.amount;
  return acc;
}, {});
console.log(JSON.stringify(grouped));

const users = [
  { name: 'Ana', age: 17 },
  { name: 'Ben', age: 25 },
  { name: 'Cy', age: 30 },
];

const firstAdult = users.find(u => u.age >= 18);
console.log(firstAdult.name);
console.log(users.some(u => u.age < 18));
console.log(users.every(u => u.age >= 18));

const numbers = [10, 2, 1, 20];
const defaultSorted = [...numbers].sort();
const numericSorted = [...numbers].sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(JSON.stringify(defaultSorted));
console.log(JSON.stringify(numericSorted));

interviewQuestions.answer:
.map() returns a new array containing the result of calling a transformation function on every element, leaving the original array unchanged. .forEach() also calls a function on every element, but always returns undefined. It exists purely for running side effects, such as logging, pushing into an external array, or updating a counter, not for producing a new collection.

```js
const nums = [1, 2, 3];
const doubled = nums.map(n => n * 2);
console.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6] -- a new array

const result = nums.forEach(n => n * 2);
console.log(result); // undefined -- forEach does not build anything
```

The practical rule: if you need the transformed values as a new array, use .map(). If you are just performing an action for each element and do not need a return value, use .forEach(). Using .forEach() and trying to manually push() into an array you declared outside the loop works, but it is considered less idiomatic than just using .map() directly for that purpose.

By default, .sort() converts every element to a string and compares them lexicographically, dictionary order, not numerically. That means '10' sorts before '2', because '1', the first character of '10', comes before '2' alphabetically, even though 10 is numerically larger than 2.

```js
console.log([10, 2, 1].sort());
// [1, 10, 2] -- sorted as strings, not numbers!
```

The fix is to pass an explicit comparator function that tells .sort() how to order two elements numerically: return a negative number if the first should come first, a positive number if the second should, or zero if they are equal.

```js
console.log([10, 2, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b));
// [1, 2, 10] -- correctly sorted numerically
```

It is also worth remembering that .sort() mutates the array in place and returns the same reference, so if you need to preserve the original order elsewhere, sort a copy: [...array].sort(comparator).

.reduce() is general enough to implement almost any other array transformation, including .map(), because .map() is really just folding each element into a new array after transforming it.

```js
function mapWithReduce(array, transformFn) {
  return array.reduce((accumulator, currentValue, index, arr) => {
    accumulator.push(transformFn(currentValue, index, arr));
    return accumulator;
  }, []);
}

const doubled = mapWithReduce([1, 2, 3], n => n * 2);
console.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6]
```

Walking through it: reduce starts with an empty array [] as the accumulator. For every element, it calls transformFn on that element, mirroring exactly what .map()'s callback signature provides (value, index, and the original array), pushes the result onto the accumulator, and returns the accumulator so it carries forward into the next iteration. By the time reduce finishes, the accumulator holds every transformed value, in order, exactly what .map() would have produced.

This exercise is a common interview question because it demonstrates that reduce is the most fundamental, general-purpose of the array iteration methods. map, filter, and even forEach can all be expressed in terms of it, which is why some functional programming libraries build their entire array toolkit on top of a single reduce primitive.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## Object Literal Syntax, Shorthand Properties, and Computed Keys

Object literals ({ ... }) are the most common way to create structured data in JavaScript. ES2015 added several syntax improvements that make them more concise and expressive.

Property shorthand lets you skip repeating a variable name as both the key and the value when they match:

```js
const name = 'Nora';
const age = 28;
const user = { name, age }; // same as { name: name, age: age }
```

Method shorthand removes the need for the function keyword when defining methods on an object:

```js
const calculator = {
  add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
  },
};
```

Computed property names let you use an expression, not just a literal identifier, as a key, by wrapping it in square brackets:

```js
const field = 'email';
const formValues = {
  [field]: 'nora@example.com',
  [`${field}Verified`]: true,
};
```

This is especially useful when building objects dynamically, such as converting form data, API responses, or query parameters into a structured object where key names are not known until runtime.

Together, these features make object literals far more compact than pre-ES2015 JavaScript, where you would need to assign each property on a separate line after creating the base object.

codeExamples.code:
const name = 'Nora';
const age = 28;
const user = {
  name,
  age,
  greet() {
    return `Hi, I'm ${this.name}`;
  },
};
console.log(JSON.stringify({ name: user.name, age: user.age }));
console.log(user.greet());

function buildField(key, value) {
  return {
    [key]: value,
    [`${key}Length`]: String(value).length,
  };
}
console.log(JSON.stringify(buildField('code', 'A1B2')));

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## Destructuring Arrays and Objects

Destructuring lets you unpack values from arrays or properties from objects into distinct variables in a single, declarative statement, instead of accessing them one by one.

Array destructuring matches by position:

```js
const [first, second] = [10, 20, 30];
// first = 10, second = 20 (30 is ignored)
```

You can skip elements with empty commas, collect the remainder with a rest pattern, and provide defaults for missing positions:

```js
const [a, , c = 99] = [1, undefined];
// a = 1, c = 99 -- default used because index 1 is undefined
```

Object destructuring matches by property name, and supports two features constantly seen in real-world code: renaming and default values:

```js
const { name: userName, role = 'guest' } = { name: 'Theo' };
// userName = 'Theo', role = 'guest' -- default used, role was not present
```

Destructuring is especially powerful in function parameters, letting you pull just the fields you need directly out of an options object:

```js
function createUser({ name, role = 'member' }) {
  return `${name} joins as ${role}`;
}
```

Nested destructuring is also supported for deeply structured data, by mirroring the shape of the source object or array in the pattern on the left-hand side of the =. Destructuring does not just make code shorter, it documents, right at the call site or assignment, exactly which fields a function or block of code actually depends on.

codeExamples.code:
const [first, , third = 99, ...rest] = [1, 2, undefined, 4, 5];
console.log(first);
console.log(third);
console.log(JSON.stringify(rest));

const response = {
  data: { id: 1, name: 'Theo' },
  meta: { page: 1 },
};

const {
  data: { name: userName },
  meta: { page, perPage = 10 },
} = response;

console.log(userName);
console.log(page);
console.log(perPage);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Property shorthand lets you omit the key: value repetition when a variable's name is identical to the property name you want on the object. Instead of writing { name: name, age: age }, you can simply write { name, age }, and JavaScript infers the key names from the variable names automatically.

```js
function createUser(name, age) {
  return { name, age }; // shorthand, same as { name: name, age: age }
}
console.log(createUser('Omar', 34));
// { name: 'Omar', age: 34 }
```

This is especially common when returning data from a function, building an object from several local variables, or constructing a component's props object. It reduces visual noise and repetition, making it immediately clear that the object's keys are meant to directly mirror the names of existing variables in scope, without having to scan both sides of each key: value pair to confirm they match.

You can destructure directly in the function's parameter list, combining object destructuring with default values so that callers only need to supply the options they care about, while everything else falls back sensibly.

```js
function createServer({ port = 3000, host = 'localhost', verbose = false } = {}) {
  console.log(`Starting server at ${host}:${port}`);
  if (verbose) console.log('Verbose logging enabled');
}

createServer(); // Starting server at localhost:3000
createServer({ port: 8080 }); // Starting server at localhost:8080
```

Notice the = {} at the very end of the destructuring pattern. This is important: it provides a default for the entire parameter object, so calling createServer() with no arguments at all does not throw a cannot-destructure-property-of-undefined error. Without that outer default, destructuring a missing argument would fail immediately, since you cannot destructure undefined.

This pattern is extremely common for configuration-style function signatures, since it documents the available options directly in the function signature and avoids large if/undefined-check blocks inside the function body.

Computed property names let you use an arbitrary expression, wrapped in square brackets, as an object key instead of a fixed identifier or string literal. The expression is evaluated once, converted to a string (or used as-is if it is a Symbol), and used as the property's key.

```js
const fieldName = 'email';
const user = {
  [fieldName]: 'ana@example.com',
  [`${fieldName}Verified`]: true,
};
console.log(JSON.stringify(user));
// {"email":"ana@example.com","emailVerified":true}
```

A practical use case is building an object whose keys are not known until runtime, for example converting an array of form fields into a lookup object keyed by each field's name property, tallying counts into a dictionary keyed by a dynamic category, or generating multiple related keys, like email and emailVerified above, from a single variable. Before computed property names existed, you had to create the object first and then assign each dynamic key as a separate statement, obj[fieldName] = value, which was more verbose and could not be done inline within a single object literal.

codeExamples.code:
const base = [1, 2, 3];
const combined = [...base, 4, 5];
const copy = [...base];
copy.push(99);
console.log(JSON.stringify(combined));
console.log(JSON.stringify(base));
console.log(JSON.stringify(copy));

const defaults = { theme: 'light', fontSize: 14 };
const overrides = { fontSize: 18 };
const config = { ...defaults, ...overrides };
console.log(JSON.stringify(config));
console.log(Math.max(...[4, 8, 15]));

