## Rest Parameters, Shallow Copying, and Merging

Rest parameters use the same ... syntax as spread but appear in a function's parameter list or on the left side of a destructuring assignment, where they collect multiple values into a single real array (or object), rather than expanding one.

```js
function sum(...numbers) {
  return numbers.reduce((total, n) => total + n, 0);
}
sum(1, 2, 3, 4); // numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

Rest can also be combined with named parameters or destructuring to collect the leftovers:

```js
function logFirst(first, ...others) {
  console.log(first, others);
}

const { id, ...otherFields } = { id: 1, name: 'Rey', role: 'admin' };
// otherFields = { name: 'Rey', role: 'admin' }
```

Because spread-based copies and merges are shallow, understanding the distinction between shallow and deep copying matters a lot in practice:

- A shallow copy duplicates the top-level structure (the array itself, or the object's own keys), but any nested objects/arrays inside are still the same reference as in the original.
- A deep copy recursively duplicates every nested level, so nothing is shared.

```js
const original = { profile: { city: 'Austin' } };
const shallow = { ...original };
shallow.profile.city = 'Dallas';
console.log(original.profile.city); // 'Dallas' -- the nested object was shared!
```

For true deep copies, use structuredClone() (built into modern Node.js and browsers) or a recursive utility. Spread alone is not sufficient whenever your data has nested objects or arrays that might be mutated later.

codeExamples.code:
function sum(first, ...rest) {
  return first + rest.reduce((total, n) => total + n, 0);
}
console.log(sum(1, 2, 3, 4));

const { id, ...otherFields } = { id: 1, name: 'Rey', role: 'admin' };
console.log(id);
console.log(JSON.stringify(otherFields));

const original = { profile: { city: 'Austin' } };
const shallowCopy = { ...original };
shallowCopy.profile.city = 'Dallas';
console.log(original.profile.city);
console.log(shallowCopy.profile.city);
console.log(original.profile === shallowCopy.profile);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Spread and rest use identical ... syntax but perform opposite operations, and which one you are looking at depends entirely on where it appears.

- Spread expands an iterable or object into its individual elements/properties. It appears on the right-hand side of an assignment, inside an array/object literal, or as a function call argument.
- Rest collects multiple individual values into a single array (or object). It appears in a function's parameter list, or on the left-hand side of a destructuring pattern.

```js
// Spread: expanding
const arr = [1, 2, 3];
console.log(Math.max(...arr)); // spreads array into separate arguments

// Rest: collecting
function sum(...numbers) {   // collects arguments into an array
  return numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}
```

A simple way to remember it: spread always appears where a collection is being consumed or expanded (a function call, an array/object literal being built), while rest always appears where a collection is being produced or captured (a parameter list, or the left side of a destructuring assignment).

To update a nested property immutably, you need to spread every level of the object on the path to the property you are changing, not just the top level, otherwise you end up mutating a nested object that is still shared with the original.

```js
const state = {
  user: { name: 'Lia', address: { city: 'Austin', zip: '73301' } },
};

// Correct: spread every level down to the field being changed
const nextState = {
  ...state,
  user: {
    ...state.user,
    address: {
      ...state.user.address,
      city: 'Dallas',
    },
  },
};
console.log(state.user.address.city);     // 'Austin' -- original untouched
console.log(nextState.user.address.city); // 'Dallas'
```

If you forget to spread an intermediate level, for example writing a new user object that only contains the updated address without spreading state.user itself, you silently drop every other field that was on user, like name, because you replaced the whole user object instead of merging into it. This is a very common and subtle bug in reducers (Redux, useReducer) where state updates must remain immutable for change detection to work correctly.

codeExamples.code:
// Assume this HTML exists on the page:
// <ul id="list">
//   <li class="item">Apple</li>
//   <li class="item">Banana</li>
// </ul>
const list = document.getElementById("list");
const firstItem = document.querySelector(".item");

console.log(list.id);
console.log(firstItem.textContent);

const staticList = document.querySelectorAll(".item");       // static NodeList
const liveCollection = document.getElementsByClassName("item"); // live HTMLCollection
console.log(staticList.length, liveCollection.length);

const newLi = document.createElement("li");
newLi.className = "item";
newLi.textContent = "Cherry";
document.getElementById("list").appendChild(newLi);

console.log(staticList.length, liveCollection.length);

// Assume this HTML exists on the page:
// <div id="card">
//   <h2>Title</h2>
//   <p class="desc">Description text</p>
// </div>
const desc = document.querySelector(".desc");

console.log(desc.parentElement.id);
console.log(desc.parentElement.children.length);
console.log(desc.closest("#card") === desc.parentElement);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Both methods return a single DOM element, but they differ in how you specify what to find and in raw performance.

- `getElementById("id")` takes a plain id string (without the `#` prefix) and looks up the element directly using the browser's internal id index. It only matches the `id` attribute.
- `querySelector("selector")` takes any valid CSS selector — an id (`#id`), a class (`.class`), an attribute selector (`[data-x]`), a combinator (`.parent > .child`), and so on — and returns the **first** element in document order that matches.

Because `getElementById` is a direct lookup rather than a general selector match, it is typically faster, but the difference is negligible outside of performance-critical, tight loops. In modern code `querySelector` is often preferred for its flexibility, while `getElementById` remains a clear, fast choice when you already know you want a uniquely identified element.

```js
const a = document.getElementById("main-header");
const b = document.querySelector("#main-header");
// a and b reference the same element here
```

Repeatedly calling `querySelector` / `querySelectorAll` inside hot code paths (scroll handlers, render loops, frequent event callbacks) forces the browser to re-walk matching parts of the DOM tree each time, which adds up in large, dynamic applications. A few strategies help:

- **Cache references** to elements that don't get replaced, typically right after they're created or on initial render, and only re-query when you know the DOM structure actually changed.
- **Scope queries** to a known subtree (`container.querySelectorAll(...)` instead of `document.querySelectorAll(...)`) so the browser has far fewer nodes to examine.
- **Use event delegation** (one listener on a stable ancestor) instead of attaching and re-attaching listeners to many individual, frequently re-created child elements — this avoids both repeated querying and repeated listener setup/teardown.
- Prefer `closest(selector)` over manually walking `parentElement` in a loop when you need to find an ancestor matching a pattern. `closest()` is implemented natively and is both more concise and typically faster than a hand-rolled `while (el && !el.matches(selector)) el = el.parentElement;` loop, and it correctly includes the starting element itself in the match.

```js
// Manual traversal (works, but verbose and easy to get wrong)
let el = event.target;
while (el && !el.classList.contains("card")) {
  el = el.parentElement;
}

// Equivalent, using closest()
const card = event.target.closest(".card");
```

The general principle is: minimize the number of full-tree queries, scope them as narrowly as possible, and prefer the browser's native traversal APIs over hand-written loops, since they are both clearer and well-optimized internally.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## Listening for Events

`element.addEventListener(type, handler, options)` is the standard way to react to user interaction and other browser events (clicks, key presses, form submissions, network state changes, and more). Unlike the older `element.onclick = handler` style, `addEventListener` lets you attach **multiple** handlers for the same event type on the same element without overwriting each other.

Every handler receives an **event object** as its first argument, which carries useful information:

- `event.type` — the event name, e.g. `"click"`.
- `event.target` — the exact element the event originated from.
- `event.currentTarget` — the element the listener is attached to (useful once bubbling is involved).
- `event.preventDefault()` — cancels the browser's default action (e.g. following a link, submitting a form).
- `event.stopPropagation()` — stops the event from continuing to bubble or capture further.

To stop listening, call `element.removeEventListener(type, handler)` — but this only works if you pass the **same function reference** that was originally registered; anonymous inline arrow functions can't be removed this way because a new function expression never equals a previously registered one.

```js
function onClick(event) {
  console.log("clicked", event.target.tagName);
}
button.addEventListener("click", onClick);
// later...
button.removeEventListener("click", onClick);
```

This pattern — named function references for anything you might need to remove later — is a good habit to build early, since it avoids a common class of "my listener won't detach" bugs.

codeExamples.code:
const button = document.querySelector("#submit-btn");

function handleClick(event) {
  console.log("Button clicked!", event.type);
}

button.addEventListener("click", handleClick);
button.click(); // simulate a user click

button.removeEventListener("click", handleClick);
button.click(); // handler is no longer attached

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## How Events Travel Through the DOM

When an event fires on an element, it doesn't just run handlers on that element — it travels through the DOM tree in three phases:

1. **Capturing phase** — the event travels from `window` down to the target element's parent.
2. **Target phase** — the event reaches the actual target element.
3. **Bubbling phase** — the event travels back up from the target to `window`.

By default, `addEventListener(type, handler)` listens during the **bubbling** phase. Passing `{ capture: true }` as a third argument listens during the capturing phase instead.

This bubbling behavior enables a powerful pattern called **event delegation**: instead of attaching a separate listener to every child element (which is wasteful and breaks when children are added dynamically), you attach **one** listener to a stable parent and inspect `event.target` to figure out which child was actually interacted with.

```js
document.getElementById("menu").addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  if (event.target.matches("li")) {
    console.log("Clicked:", event.target.textContent);
  }
});
```

This single listener keeps working even if new `<li>` elements are added to `#menu` later, because the listener lives on the parent, not on each individual item.

Finally, two methods let you control event flow:

- `event.preventDefault()` cancels the browser's built-in behavior for the event (e.g., a link navigating, a form submitting) without affecting propagation.
- `event.stopPropagation()` stops the event from continuing to bubble (or capture) to ancestor elements, without affecting the default action.

They solve different problems and are often used independently, though sometimes together.

codeExamples.code:
// HTML: <ul id="menu"><li data-id="1">Home</li><li data-id="2">About</li></ul>
document.getElementById("menu").addEventListener("click", function (event) {
  if (event.target.tagName === "LI") {
    console.log("Clicked item id:", event.target.dataset.id);
  }
});

// Simulate a user clicking the second <li>
document.querySelectorAll("li")[1].dispatchEvent(new Event("click", { bubbles: true }));

document.querySelector("a.link").addEventListener("click", function (event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  event.stopPropagation();
  console.log("Link click intercepted, default navigation cancelled");
});

interviewQuestions.answer:
`addEventListener(type, handler)` registers a function to run whenever the given event type fires on an element. Its key advantage over the older `element.onclick = handler` style is that you can attach **multiple independent handlers** for the same event type on the same element — each call to `addEventListener` adds another handler rather than replacing the previous one.

With `element.onclick = handlerA` followed later by `element.onclick = handlerB`, `handlerB` silently overwrites `handlerA`; only one `onclick` handler can ever exist at a time, because it's just a property assignment. `addEventListener`, by contrast, maintains an internal list of listeners.

`addEventListener` also supports extra options like `{ once: true }` (auto-remove after firing once), `{ capture: true }` (listen during the capturing phase), and `{ passive: true }` (hint that the listener won't call `preventDefault`, improving scroll performance), none of which the `onclick` property style can express.

```js
button.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("handler A"));
button.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("handler B"));
// both A and B run on click
```

When an event occurs on a DOM element, it travels through three phases: **capturing** (from the `window` down to the target's parent), **target** (the event reaches the actual element interacted with), and **bubbling** (back up from the target to `window`).

By default, `addEventListener(type, handler)` registers the handler for the **bubbling** phase (the third, optional argument defaults to `false`, meaning 'do not use capture'). Passing `true` or `{ capture: true }` as the third argument instead registers the handler for the **capturing** phase, so it runs on the way down, before the event reaches its target.

In the vast majority of applications, the default bubbling behavior is what you want, because it enables event delegation: a listener on an ancestor element can react to events from descendants as they bubble up. Capturing is used far less often, typically for scenarios where you need to intercept an event before a descendant's own handler has a chance to run or to stop propagation early, such as implementing modal "click outside to close" behavior or certain analytics/logging hooks that must observe every event regardless of what descendant handlers do.

```js
parent.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("capture"), { capture: true });
child.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("target"));
parent.addEventListener("click", () => console.log("bubble"));
// Clicking child logs: capture, target, bubble
```

Event delegation is a pattern where, instead of attaching a listener to every individual child element you care about, you attach a **single** listener to a shared, stable ancestor and rely on event bubbling to let that one listener observe events from any descendant. Inside the handler, you check `event.target` (and often `event.target.closest(selector)`) to determine which specific descendant actually triggered the event, and respond accordingly.

This has two major benefits:

1. **Performance and memory**: attaching one listener instead of hundreds or thousands means far less memory overhead and far less setup/teardown cost, which matters a lot for large lists, tables, or grids.
2. **Works with dynamic content**: if new children are added to the container after the listener was attached (e.g., rows added via `appendChild`, or re-rendered list items), the delegated listener handles events from them automatically — there's no need to remember to attach a new listener to every new element.

```js
const list = document.getElementById("todo-list");

list.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const item = event.target.closest("li");
  if (!item) return; // click wasn't on or inside a list item
  if (event.target.matches(".delete-btn")) {
    item.remove();
  }
});

// Adding a new <li> later still works with the delegate listener,
// with no need to call addEventListener again:
const newItem = document.createElement("li");
newItem.innerHTML = 'New task <button class="delete-btn">Delete</button>';
list.appendChild(newItem);
```

The main caveat is that delegation only works for events that actually bubble (most do — click, input, keydown — but a few, like `focus` and `blur` in their basic form, do not, though `focusin`/`focusout` are their bubbling equivalents).

codeExamples.code:
// HTML: <ul id="list"></ul>
const list = document.getElementById("list");

const li = document.createElement("li");
li.textContent = "New Item";
list.appendChild(li);
console.log(list.children.length);

li.remove();
console.log(list.children.length);

const para = document.querySelector("#msg");
para.textContent = "Hello World";
para.classList.add("highlight");
para.style.color = "blue";

console.log(para.textContent, para.classList.contains("highlight"), para.style.color);

const container = document.querySelector("#container");
container.innerHTML = "<strong>Bold</strong> text";

console.log(container.children.length, container.children[0].tagName);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Creating and inserting an element is a two-step process: first create the element in memory, then attach it somewhere inside the live document tree.

1. Call `document.createElement("tagName")` to create a detached element.
2. Optionally set its content, attributes, or classes.
3. Insert it into the document using a method like `appendChild`, `append`, `prepend`, or `insertBefore` on the desired parent element.

An element created with `createElement` does not appear on the page at all until it's actually inserted into a node that is part of `document`.

```js
const card = document.createElement("div");
card.className = "card";
card.textContent = "I'm a new card";
document.body.appendChild(card);
// Only now does the card actually render on the page
```

If you need to add several new elements together, it's more efficient to assemble them inside a `DocumentFragment` first, then append the fragment once, rather than calling `appendChild` on the live document repeatedly.

Every time you change something that affects layout (inserting/removing elements, changing dimensions, reading certain layout-triggering properties like `offsetHeight`), the browser may need to recompute styles, re-run layout, and repaint the affected parts of the page. Doing this repeatedly, one tiny change at a time, in a tight loop can cause the browser to perform far more work than necessary — often called **layout thrashing**, especially when reads and writes of layout-affecting properties are interleaved.

Several techniques help minimize this cost:

- **Batch DOM writes together**, and batch reads together, rather than interleaving them. Reading `element.offsetHeight` right after writing a style forces the browser to flush pending layout work immediately instead of batching it.
- **Use a `DocumentFragment`** to build up a subtree off-screen, then insert it into the live document in a single operation, instead of calling `appendChild` repeatedly on the live DOM.
- **Detach an element before making many changes to it** (e.g., clone it, modify the clone, then swap it back in, or temporarily set `display: none`), so the browser doesn't need to recompute layout for each intermediate change.
- **Use `requestAnimationFrame`** to schedule visual updates in sync with the browser's natural repaint cycle rather than firing them immediately and repeatedly.
- For very large lists, consider **virtualization** — only rendering the DOM nodes that are actually visible in the viewport rather than all of them.

```js
// Inefficient: triggers a reflow on each iteration due to interleaved read/write
items.forEach((item) => {
  const el = document.createElement("li");
  el.textContent = item;
  list.appendChild(el); // write directly to the live DOM, one at a time
});

// Better: batch all writes into a fragment, then insert once
const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
items.forEach((item) => {
  const el = document.createElement("li");
  el.textContent = item;
  fragment.appendChild(el);
});
list.appendChild(fragment); // single DOM write
```

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## Running Code Later

A **callback** is simply a function passed into another function to be called later, often once some operation finishes. It's the oldest and most fundamental pattern for asynchronous JavaScript, underlying timers, event listeners, and the original Node.js I/O APIs.

`setTimeout(callback, delay)` is a classic example: it schedules `callback` to run after at least `delay` milliseconds have passed, without blocking the rest of the program. Importantly, even `setTimeout(fn, 0)` does **not** run `fn` immediately — it still waits for the current synchronous code to finish executing, because JavaScript is single-threaded and the scheduled callback is placed in a queue (the **macrotask queue**) that is only processed once the call stack is empty.

```js
console.log("first");
setTimeout(() => console.log("scheduled"), 0);
console.log("second");
// Logs: first, second, scheduled — in that order
```

Callbacks aren't limited to timers — they're also how you pass custom behavior into array methods (`array.map(callback)`), event listeners (`addEventListener("click", callback)`), and many other APIs. The common thread is **inversion of control**: you hand a function to someone else's code and trust it to call your function at the right time, with the right arguments — which is a powerful pattern, but also means you depend on that other code behaving correctly.

codeExamples.code:
function greet(name, callback) {
  callback(`Hello, ${name}!`);
}

greet("Alice", (message) => console.log(message));

console.log("Before timeout");
setTimeout(() => console.log("Inside setTimeout"), 0);
console.log("After timeout call");

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## When Callbacks Get Out of Hand

When several asynchronous steps must happen in sequence, and each one depends on the result of the last, callbacks naturally end up nested inside each other. As the number of steps grows, this produces deeply indented, hard-to-read code informally known as **"callback hell"** or the **"pyramid of doom"**:

```js
step1((result1) => {
  step2(result1, (result2) => {
    step3(result2, (result3) => {
      // ...and so on, drifting further right each time
    });
  });
});
```

Beyond the visual indentation problem, callback hell makes error handling repetitive (each nested callback typically needs its own error check) and makes the overall control flow — what runs in what order, and what happens if something fails partway through — difficult to follow. This was one of the major motivations for introducing Promises and later `async`/`await` into JavaScript.

Before Promises existed, Node.js established a convention called the **error-first callback**: every asynchronous callback's first parameter is reserved for an error (or `null` if there was none), and subsequent parameters carry the actual result.

```js
fs.readFile("data.txt", (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error("Something went wrong:", err.message);
    return;
  }
  console.log("File contents:", data);
});
```

This convention made error handling at least *consistent* across Node's built-in APIs, even if it didn't solve the nesting problem — you still had to remember to check `err` in every single callback, and forgetting to do so was (and still is) a common source of silently swallowed errors.

codeExamples.code:
const fileSystem = {
  readFileAsync(path, callback) {
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (path === "missing.txt") {
        callback(new Error("File not found"));
      } else {
        callback(null, "File contents here");
      }
    }, 0);
  },
};

fileSystem.readFileAsync("data.txt", (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.log("Error:", err.message);
  } else {
    console.log("Data:", data);
  }
});

interviewQuestions.answer:
A callback is a function that is passed as an argument to another function, with the expectation that it will be invoked (called back) at some point — either synchronously, right away, or asynchronously, once some operation completes. Callbacks are a fundamental building block for handling asynchronous work in JavaScript, since the language itself is single-threaded and needs some way to express "run this code once that other thing finishes."

Callbacks aren't only used for asynchronous code — synchronous APIs like array methods use them too:

```js
// Synchronous callback
[1, 2, 3].forEach((num) => console.log(num * 2));

// Asynchronous callback
setTimeout(() => console.log("This runs later"), 1000);
```

In both cases, the key idea is the same: you hand a function to another piece of code, and that code decides when (and with what arguments) to actually invoke it.

Callback hell refers to code where multiple asynchronous operations are chained by nesting each one's callback inside the previous one's callback, producing deeply indented code that drifts further and further to the right — often called the "pyramid of doom."

```js
getUser(userId, (err, user) => {
  if (err) return handleError(err);
  getOrders(user.id, (err, orders) => {
    if (err) return handleError(err);
    getOrderDetails(orders[0].id, (err, details) => {
      if (err) return handleError(err);
      console.log(details);
    });
  });
});
```

This pattern creates several real maintenance problems:

- **Readability** suffers as indentation grows with every additional asynchronous step.
- **Error handling becomes repetitive** — each nested level typically needs its own `if (err)` check, and it's easy to forget one, silently swallowing errors.
- **Control flow is hard to reason about**, especially once branching or parallel operations are mixed in alongside sequential ones.
- **Refactoring is painful**, since reordering or inserting a step means re-nesting a large block of code.

Promises (and later `async`/`await`) were introduced largely to solve this problem, by letting sequential asynchronous steps be expressed as a flat chain (`.then().then().then()`) or as sequential `await` statements that read almost like synchronous code, with a single `try`/`catch` handling errors from any step.

The error-first callback convention (sometimes called the "Node.js callback pattern") requires that any asynchronous callback's **first parameter** be reserved for an error object (or `null`/`undefined` if the operation succeeded), with any actual result values passed as subsequent parameters: `callback(err, result)`.

```js
fs.readFile("config.json", (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    // handle the error — e.g., log it, retry, or propagate it further
    return;
  }
  // use data safely here, knowing the read succeeded
});
```

This convention was adopted broadly across Node's built-in modules (`fs`, `http`, `dns`, and many third-party libraries built the same way) for a few key reasons:

- **Consistency**: once you learned the pattern once, you could predict the signature of almost any asynchronous Node API, which made unfamiliar APIs easier to use correctly.
- **Explicit error handling**: by making the error the very first argument, it forced (or at least strongly encouraged) developers to check for it before touching the result, rather than making error handling an afterthought.
- **A single error channel**: since JavaScript has no built-in way to "throw" an error across an asynchronous boundary and have it land in a normal try/catch (an exception thrown inside a setTimeout callback, for instance, can't be caught by a try/catch wrapping the setTimeout call), passing the error explicitly as a callback argument was the practical way to propagate it.

The main downside was that this convention was only a social agreement, not something enforced by the language — if a developer forgot the `if (err)` check, the error was silently ignored, and deeply nested error-first callbacks still suffered from the general readability problems of callback hell. This is part of why Promises, which make unhandled rejections visible and support the flatter `.then()`/`.catch()` chain, eventually replaced this pattern as the primary async style, with `async`/`await` built on top of Promises providing an even more direct migration path with native try/catch support.

