## try/catch With Async Code, and Fetching Data

One major benefit of `async`/`await` is that it lets you handle Promise rejections with ordinary `try`/`catch`, the same construct you'd use for synchronous errors — instead of attaching a separate `.catch()` to a chain.

```js
async function loadSettings() {
  try {
    const response = await fetchSettings();
    return response.data;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Failed to load settings:", err.message);
    return defaultSettings;
  }
}
```

If the awaited Promise rejects, `await` effectively **throws** that rejection reason as an exception at the point of the `await`, which the surrounding `try`/`catch` can catch just like a synchronous `throw`. This unifies error handling for both synchronous and asynchronous failures within the same function.

The **Fetch API** (`fetch(url)`) is the modern, Promise-based way to make HTTP requests in the browser (and, since recent versions, in Node as well). It returns a Promise that resolves with a `Response` object once the HTTP response headers arrive — note that `fetch`'s Promise only rejects on network-level failures (like no connectivity or a CORS issue), **not** on HTTP error status codes like 404 or 500. You must check `response.ok` (or `response.status`) explicitly and throw your own error if you want 4xx/5xx responses treated as failures.

```js
async function getJson(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed with status ${response.status}`);
  }
  return response.json(); // itself returns a Promise, so it must also be awaited
}
```

This combination — `async`/`await`, `try`/`catch`, and an explicit `response.ok` check — is the standard modern pattern for robust network requests.

codeExamples.code:
function fetchData(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (url === "/invalid") {
        reject(new Error("404 Not Found"));
      } else {
        resolve({ data: "Success payload" });
      }
    }, 0);
  });
}

async function getData(url) {
  try {
    const response = await fetchData(url);
    console.log("Received:", JSON.stringify(response));
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("Caught error:", err.message);
  }
}

getData("/invalid");

async function loadUser(id) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Request failed with status ${response.status}`);
    }
    const user = await response.json();
    console.log(user.name);
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("Failed to load user:", err.message);
  }
}

loadUser(42);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Inside an `async` function, you handle errors with an ordinary `try`/`catch` block wrapped around one or more `await` expressions. If any awaited Promise rejects, `await` throws that rejection reason at the point it was awaited, and the nearest enclosing `catch` block handles it — exactly like a synchronous `throw` would.

```js
async function loadData() {
  try {
    const data = await fetchData();
    return process(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Failed:", err.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```

This is functionally equivalent to attaching a `.catch()` to an explicit Promise chain:

```js
function loadData() {
  return fetchData()
    .then((data) => process(data))
    .catch((err) => {
      console.error("Failed:", err.message);
      return null;
    });
}
```

The `try`/`catch` style tends to be preferred for sequences of multiple dependent steps, since one `catch` block can cover several `await` statements at once, mirroring how synchronous error handling already works, and it avoids having to attach a separate `.catch()` to every intermediate step in a chain. A key gotcha: if you forget the `try`/`catch` (or don't let the error propagate to a caller that catches it), a rejected Promise returned from — or awaited inside — an `async` function becomes an unhandled rejection, same as an uncaught `.catch()`-less promise chain.

If you `await` several independent asynchronous calls one after another, each `await` pauses until the previous one fully settles before the next one even **starts**, effectively serializing operations that don't actually depend on each other and adding their durations together.

```js
// Sequential: total time is roughly the SUM of all three durations
async function loadAllSequential() {
  const users = await fetchUsers();     // starts, finishes
  const posts = await fetchPosts();     // only starts after users finishes
  const comments = await fetchComments(); // only starts after posts finishes
  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

If the three calls don't depend on each other's results, you can start all of them immediately and only await their combined completion, using `Promise.all`:

```js
// Concurrent: total time is roughly the duration of the SLOWEST call
async function loadAllConcurrent() {
  const [users, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUsers(),
    fetchPosts(),
    fetchComments(),
  ]);
  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

The key technique is to call all the asynchronous functions **first**, without awaiting each one individually, so each one begins its work (e.g., its network request) right away; only then do you `await Promise.all([...])` on the resulting array of Promises. If any of them depend on another's result (e.g., you need a user's id before you can fetch their posts), they genuinely can't run concurrently and must stay sequential — the optimization only applies to operations that are truly independent of each other.

For error handling, remember that `Promise.all` rejects as soon as any one of the Promises rejects, discarding the others' results even if they would have succeeded; if partial success matters, `Promise.allSettled` is the better fit, since it waits for every Promise to settle and reports each individual outcome rather than short-circuiting.

codeExamples.code:
console.log(typeof varVariable);
var varVariable = "I'm hoisted";

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log("let i:", i), 0);
}

for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log("var j:", j), 0);
}

