## Structured Object Blueprints

ES6 introduced `class` syntax as a cleaner, more familiar way to write the same prototype-based object creation JavaScript already supported — `class` doesn't introduce a fundamentally new object model, but it does make common patterns far easier to read and write correctly.

A basic class has a `constructor` method (run automatically when you create an instance with `new`), instance methods (shared via the prototype, so they're not duplicated per instance), and can declare **static** members with the `static` keyword — properties and methods that belong to the class itself rather than to individual instances.

```js
class Rectangle {
  static unit = "px";

  constructor(width, height) {
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
  }

  area() {
    return this.width * this.height;
  }

  static describe() {
    return `Measured in ${Rectangle.unit}`;
  }
}

const r = new Rectangle(4, 5);
r.area();              // 20 — an instance method
Rectangle.describe();  // static method, called on the class itself, not an instance
```

For inheritance, `class Dog extends Animal` makes `Dog` a **subclass** of `Animal`, inheriting its instance methods and properties. Inside a subclass's constructor, `super(...)` must be called before using `this`, since it invokes the parent class's constructor to properly initialize the inherited state. Inside an overridden method, `super.methodName()` calls the parent class's version of that method, which is useful when you want to extend rather than completely replace the inherited behavior.

Under the hood, classes are still built on JavaScript's prototype chain — `extends` sets up the prototype link automatically — but the syntax hides that machinery behind a much more approachable, conventional-looking structure.

codeExamples.code:
class Animal {
  static kingdom = "Animalia";

  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  speak() {
    return `${this.name} makes a sound.`;
  }

  static describe() {
    return `All animals belong to ${Animal.kingdom}`;
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() {
    return `${super.speak()} Specifically, ${this.name} barks.`;
  }
}

const d = new Dog("Rex");
console.log(d.speak());
console.log(Animal.describe());

interviewQuestions.answer:
Under the hood, `class Dog extends Animal` is still built on JavaScript's existing prototype chain — ES6 classes are often described as 'syntactic sugar' over the same mechanism, though with some real behavioral differences and guardrails added.

When you write `class Dog extends Animal`, the engine sets `Dog.prototype`'s internal `[[Prototype]]` to `Animal.prototype`, so instances of `Dog` can look up inherited methods through the prototype chain exactly as they could with manual prototype wiring. It also links `Dog` itself (the constructor function) to `Animal` (its internal `[[Prototype]]` points to `Animal`), which is what makes static members inheritable too — something that required extra manual work (like `Object.setPrototypeOf(Dog, Animal)`) in pre-ES6 patterns.

Before classes, the equivalent pattern looked like this:

```js
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}
Animal.prototype.speak = function () {
  return `${this.name} makes a sound.`;
};

function Dog(name) {
  Animal.call(this, name); // manually invoke the 'parent constructor'
}
Dog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype); // manually wire up the prototype chain
Dog.prototype.constructor = Dog;
```

With `class` syntax, `super(...)` inside a subclass's constructor plays the role that `Animal.call(this, name)` played above — it invokes the parent class's constructor with the correct `this`. A key difference: in a derived class (one using `extends`), you **must** call `super()` before you can access `this` at all; the engine enforces this with a runtime `ReferenceError` if you try to use `this` beforehand, because until `super()` runs, `this` is not yet initialized in a derived class's constructor. This is stricter than the old pattern, where nothing stopped you from using `this` before calling the parent constructor function, even if doing so was usually a bug.

`super.methodName()` similarly provides direct, explicit access to the parent prototype's version of a method, something that previously required more verbose and error-prone code like `Animal.prototype.speak.call(this)`.

Overall, classes don't change what's fundamentally possible in JavaScript's object model, but they remove a lot of repetitive, easy-to-get-wrong boilerplate and add helpful runtime checks (like the mandatory `super()` call) that catch mistakes the older pattern allowed silently.

