A closure is formed when a function retains access to variables from the scope it was defined in, even after the outer function that created that scope has already finished executing. This happens because JavaScript functions remember the lexical environment they were created in, not just the code they contain.

```js
function makeGreeter(greeting) {
  return function (name) {
    return `${greeting}, ${name}!`;
  };
}

const sayHello = makeGreeter("Hello");
console.log(sayHello("Alice")); // "Hello, Alice!"
```

Here, the inner function returned by `makeGreeter` continues to have access to `greeting` long after `makeGreeter("Hello")` has finished running and returned. Each call to `makeGreeter` creates a distinct closure with its own independent `greeting` value, so `makeGreeter("Hi")` would produce a completely separate greeter function unaffected by `sayHello`.

Closures are everywhere in everyday JavaScript — event handlers, callbacks passed to array methods, and factory functions that produce objects with private state all rely on this behavior, even when it isn't explicitly labeled as a 'closure.'

A 'factory function' that declares local variables and returns an object (or functions) referencing them is a common closure-based way to create private, encapsulated state. Because the variables are declared inside the factory function's scope rather than as properties on the returned object, there is no way for outside code to read or modify them directly — the only access is through whatever functions the factory explicitly returns and exposes.

```js
function createCounter(start = 0) {
  let count = start; // private: lives only in this closure

  return {
    increment() { return ++count; },
    decrement() { return --count; },
    value() { return count; },
  };
}

const counterA = createCounter();
const counterB = createCounter(100);

counterA.increment(); // 1
counterB.increment(); // 101 — completely independent of counterA
console.log(counterA.count); // undefined — 'count' was never exposed as a property
```

Each call to `createCounter` produces a fresh closure with its own independent `count` variable, so multiple counters created this way never interfere with one another — unlike, say, a single shared module-level variable would.

This pattern predates true private class fields (`#field` syntax) and is still widely used, particularly for simple state machines, caches, or modules that need to guard internal invariants (e.g., preventing a counter from ever going below zero) by only exposing controlled methods rather than a raw, freely-mutable property.
